  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday: Low Potential for Showers North of I-94, Breezy\nTuesday\n\nRather benign weather continues across the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley today into Tuesday. The exception is the low \npotential (10-30%) for some scattered showers north of I-94 \ntonight as a 700hPa shortwave moves through the region. There\nare some saturation issues as some drier air is depicted below \n5-6kft in RRFS/RAP/HRRR soundings, but the forcing from the \naforementioned shortwave along with warm air advection at 700hPa\nand saturation above the dry layer should result in some \nmeasurable rainfall. Currently expect amounts to be around 0.1\" \nor less although the 05.12z HREF does suggest some potential for\namounts to be as high as 0.25\" (10-20%). The highest rainfall\namounts are expected across northern Wisconsin.\n\nSoutherly winds increase Tuesday morning as a 850hPa low level \njet of 35-40KT moves into the region, leading to some breezy \nconditions across the region. Thinking gusts upwards of 30-35mph\nare most probable, especially across southeast Minnesota. That \nsaid, the 05.12z HREF/REFS do suggest 50-70% probabilities for \ngusts to exceed 35mph though this is in contrast to the 05.13z \nNBM which has 10-20% probabilities for the same scenario. \n\nTemperatures also increase Tuesday given southerly flow and \nwarm air advection at 850hPa, resulting in highs topping out in \nthe low to mid 70s as diurnal and mechanical mixing bring the \nwarmer temperatures aloft to the surface. Winds diminish Tuesday\nafternoon/evening as the low level jet moves eastward and a \nsurface front moves through the region. \n\nWednesday - This Weekend: Dry, Near to Slightly Above Normal \nTemperatures\n\nUpper level northwest flow develops over the Upper Midwest \nthrough the rest of the week as longwave ridging builds across \nthe western United States and longwave troughing situates over \nthe eastern Great Lakes region. Little in the way of synoptic \nforcing appears evident along the mean northwest flow through \nthe weekend, so precipitation potential is very low, topping out\naround 10-20% on Sunday. Along with the lack of synoptic \nforcing, little airmass change is expected through at least \nFriday, so temperatures are expected to remain fairly steady \nstate behind the aforementioned surface front. Highs generally \nremain in the mid 60s to low 70s through Friday with little \nvariation in the interquartile range of the various ensemble \nsuites. \n\nSome increased temperature variation in the ensembles comes \nthis weekend as the upper ridge begins to shift eastward and \nmodel guidance suggests a longwave trough moves onto the west \ncoast of the United States. An overall warming trend is depicted\nduring this time as surface cyclogenesis occurs over the \nnorthern Great Plains associated with said trough and southerly \nflow advects warmer air into the Upper Midwest, though timing \nand placement variations of the synoptic features leads to \nuncertainty in the degree of warming and what temperatures will\nultimately end up being. The 25th-75th percentiles for \ntemperatures range about 10 degrees, though generally end up in\nthe 70s. A few outlier members suggest temperatures may end up \nwarmer or colder, but the overall signal is towards a bit of a \nwarm up this weekend.",
